# Build output. `.cargo/config.toml` puts the workspace's build directory at
# `Target`, and the anchored entry is the one that matters: a bare `Target/`
# would match that directory name at any depth.
/Target/

# No `node_modules/` and no `.turbo/` rule. The TypeScript toolchain was deleted
# with the packages it served, so nothing creates either directory - and an
# unanchored ignore rule for a directory nothing creates is a rule that hides the
# day something starts creating one.

# The working archive: research captures, review bundles, notes and the tooling
# that made them. It is the owner's scratch rather than repository content, and
# it stays on disk. It is untracked because a capture records the page it came
# from - an account id, an organisation, a person's name - and a tracked file
# may name none of those.
.hermes/

# Local secrets: any API key, any token. A credential in this repository deserves
# a rule that travels with the repository rather than one that depends on a
# machine's global ignore.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Operating-system droppings.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
__pycache__/

# The agent instruction file is source, not scratch: a global ignore for
# `AGENTS.md` must not take it out of this tree.
!AGENTS.md

# A lock file is machine state, not repository content. It is generated locally and
# by any CI job that needs one; it is never committed, in this repository or any
# other. `--locked` therefore cannot appear in a workflow step - a fresh checkout
# has no lock file for the flag to match - and a cache key hashes the manifests.
Cargo.lock
/target/
